    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d574">

                <pb facs="0662" n="569" sameAs="frus:frus1935v04/pg569"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">816.51C39/334</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in El
                        Salvador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Corrigan</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 80</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-25">February 25, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: It has been noted that paragraph (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">e</hi>) of Section 1 of Article IV of the Deposit
                    Agreement of March 24, 1932,<anchor n="27" xml:id="d574-f1"/> under which owners
                    of Salvadoran bonds have deposited them with the Bondholders Protective
                    Committee for the Republic of El Salvador, empowers the Committee to <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Amend, modify or adjust the provisions for payment of the principal
                            and/or interest of the Bonds, or some or all of the provisions of <pb
                                facs="0663" n="570" xml:id="pg570"/> the Loan Contract<anchor n="28"
                                xml:id="d574-f2"/> or other instrument, or the rights or powers of
                            any agent or agency bearing upon or connected with the collection of the
                            pledged Customs Revenues, or any tribunal or procedure in respect
                            thereof.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>The Department takes it for granted that the Government of El Salvador, in the
                    event it reaches a revised agreement with the Committee referred to, will wish
                    to omit from the revised agreement any reference involving the Government of the
                    United States or any of its officials.</p>
                <p>The Department in that case would not, of course, offer any objection to such
                    omission.</p>
                <p>You are authorized to set forth the Department’s attitude as outlined, informally
                    and discreetly, to the appropriate officials of the Government of El Salvador
                    should the question arise.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William
                            Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d574-f1" n="27" xml:id="d574-f1-content">
                <p>Republic of El Salvador, <hi rendition="#italic">Deposit Agreement Dated as of
                        March 24, 1982</hi> (n. p., n. d.).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d574-f2" n="28" xml:id="d574-f2-content">
                <p>For correspondence concerning the loan contract entered into by the Government of
                    El Salvador and Mr. Minor C. Keith on June 24, 1922, see <ref
                        target="frus1922v02#pg_885"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1922, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp. 885</ref> ff. For the
                    readjustment of this loan contract, see Foreign Bondholders Protective Council,
                    Inc., <hi rendition="#italic">Annual Report, 1986</hi> (New York, 1937),
                    p.373.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
